{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RCA (rebar-debug Phase 1, runtime-confirmed)\n\nRoot cause — a specific changeable artifact: the PRE-LOCK early-out in `sync.reconverge` (src/rebar/_store/sync.py) called `_lock.check_no_rebase_in_progress(tracker)` BEFORE acquiring the store write lock. `check_no_rebase_in_progress` (src/rebar/_store/lock.py:175) raises `RebaseGuard` on any `MERGE_HEAD`. When a peer holds the write lock and Git has created a transient `MERGE_HEAD` during the lock-owned non-fast-forward push-recovery merge, the pre-lock check saw that marker, logged 'tickets sync skipped — tracker in rebase/merge recovery state (run: rebar fsck-recover)', and returned WITHOUT ever waiting for the lock — misclassifying the lock holder's own in-progress merge as an abandoned recovery state and skipping the reconvergence round.\n\nRuntime confirmation: the reporter's deterministic two-process harness (h1_stage3.py, SHA-256 e65c356b…) reproduced hypothesis_1=TRUE on origin/main (dddaa60): while MERGE_HEAD existed and the write lock was held by the push process (pid matched), the peer reconverge emitted the WARNING from funcName 'reconverge' at sync.py:300 and returned in 0.0001s (waited_for_write_lock=false); after the merge released, the push succeeded, MERGE_HEAD cleared, lock cleared, local==remote.\n\nAttribution (caused_by): cindery-lithe-acaciarat (e699-7df5-bd51-40f6) moved the push-retry recovery merge UNDER the write lock and added the in-lock `check_no_rebase_in_progress` re-check, but its plan explicitly preserved the pre-existing pre-lock check as an early exit. Before that change the push-retry merge ran unlocked, so no lock-owned MERGE_HEAD existed for the pre-lock check to misclassify; the change introduced the interaction that made the previously-correct pre-lock classification wrong. Close class: regression.\n\nFix: remove the pre-lock recovery-state early-out so classification is authoritative only after the write lock is acquired. Fetch stays outside the lock; the in-lock `check_no_rebase_in_progress` guard in `_do_reconverge` still refuses a genuinely stranded marker (one that survives lock acquisition) and directs the operator to rebar fsck-recover. RED→GREEN: tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py::test_reconverge_waits_for_a_lock_owned_push_recovery_merge (barrier-based, RED on the pre-lock warning before the fix, GREEN after) and tests/unit/test_store_sync_reconverge.py::test_reconverge_refuses_a_stranded_merge_marker_after_lock_acquisition (preserves stranded refusal via the in-lock guard). Harness now reports hypothesis_1=FALSE."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787938503625808001,"uuid":"fc657692-f957-4fef-afa6-377b2d44a74c"}